WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.359
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4429.

2
00:00:01.359 --> 00:00:02.718
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.718 --> 00:00:03.171
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright.

4
00:00:03.171 --> 00:00:13.139
Today is March twenty-seventh, 2015 and I’m Chelsea Arseneault representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with the LSU Libraries.

5
00:00:13.139 --> 00:00:19.935
We’re working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

6
00:00:19.935 --> 00:00:24.918
Today we’re at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

7
00:00:24.918 --> 00:00:28.090
I’m here today with Mr. Barry Edwards.

8
00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:31.261
Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

9
00:00:31.261 --> 00:00:37.151
This is going to be a short interview, about fifteen to twenty minutes.

10
00:00:37.151 --> 00:00:46.665
And we’ll likely be in touch with you later to set up a longer interview if you’re interested in continuing on.

11
00:00:46.665 --> 00:00:47.118
<v BARRY EDWARDS>Okay.

12
00:00:47.118 --> 00:00:52.102
<v ARSENEAULT>So just for starters, could you please state your full name?

13
00:00:52.102 --> 00:00:53.461
<v EDWARDS>Barry Wayne Edwards.

14
00:00:53.461 --> 00:00:53.914
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

15
00:00:53.914 --> 00:00:57.086
And when and where were you born?

16
00:00:57.086 --> 00:01:01.616
<v EDWARDS>I was born April 14, 1951 in the Mossville area.

17
00:01:01.616 --> 00:01:03.882
But through Saint Patrick Hospital.

18
00:01:03.882 --> 00:01:04.335
Yeah.

19
00:01:04.335 --> 00:01:11.131
Matter of fact, from what memory has told me that they had midwives at Saint

20
00:01:11.131 --> 00:01:12.037
<c.p>Patrick’s Hospital.

21
00:01:12.037 --> 00:01:16.567
And I was one of the children that they delivered.

22
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:24.269
Matter of fact, one of the few that were actually brought to the hospital to be born.

23
00:01:24.269 --> 00:01:24.722
Yeah.

24
00:01:24.722 --> 00:01:27.441
<v ARSENEAULT>Where were people being born otherwise?

25
00:01:27.441 --> 00:01:29.706
<v EDWARDS>Us, at homes, you know.

26
00:01:29.706 --> 00:01:32.424
Everybody else was at the hospital.

27
00:01:32.424 --> 00:01:36.049
But this Mossville area, we had the midwives.

28
00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:40.127
And from there they took us to the hospital.

29
00:01:40.127 --> 00:01:42.392
<v ARSENEAULT>What are your parents’ names?

30
00:01:42.392 --> 00:01:43.751
Your parents’ names?

31
00:01:43.751 --> 00:01:46.922
<v EDWARDS>My father’s name is McKeever Edwards, Senior.

32
00:01:46.922 --> 00:01:51.000
And my mother’s name is Thelma Prater Edwards.

33
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:51.540
<v ARSENEAULT>Prater.

34
00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.318
That’s a name we hear a lot.

35
00:01:55.318 --> 00:01:55.858
<v EDWARDS>Right.

36
00:01:55.858 --> 00:01:56.398
Right.

37
00:01:56.398 --> 00:01:59.637
Majority of it has been Praters.

38
00:01:59.637 --> 00:02:01.796
It’s a bigger family.

39
00:02:01.796 --> 00:02:10.433
My great-grandfather and his brother, they originated in, around Jasper, Texas, in that area over there.

40
00:02:10.433 --> 00:02:21.229
And they left Texas, my grandfather and his brother . . . I never met his brother, didn’t know his name or anything.

41
00:02:21.229 --> 00:02:33.104
But my grandfather, my great-grandfather settled in the Sulphur area, where the other brother, he settled around, oh, shoot, up around Alexandria.

42
00:02:33.104 --> 00:02:35.803
Forest Village or Forest Hill?

43
00:02:35.803 --> 00:02:38.502
Yeah, over in that area.

44
00:02:38.502 --> 00:02:42.281
He was good with plants and all.

45
00:02:42.281 --> 00:02:57.935
But there’s a [little bit more?] story behind that about how I got to know of him but never learned his name is from one of my cousins here.

46
00:02:57.935 --> 00:03:04.413
At least one of my aunties was friends with someone in Alexandria.

47
00:03:04.413 --> 00:03:05.493
And she

48
00:03:05.493 --> 00:03:08.192
<c.p>went up there to visit.

49
00:03:08.192 --> 00:03:11.970
And she came back with a kid.

50
00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:17.368
And that’s how I knew their correlation with that great-grandfather.

51
00:03:17.368 --> 00:03:21.147
Or great-great, however that goes, you know.

52
00:03:21.147 --> 00:03:23.846
<v ARSENEAULT>What were your grandparents’ names?

53
00:03:23.846 --> 00:03:27.624
<v EDWARDS>The only one I knew was Frank.

54
00:03:27.624 --> 00:03:28.704
Frank Edwards.

55
00:03:28.704 --> 00:03:34.642
I was reading one of the deals that someone had done.

56
00:03:34.642 --> 00:03:37.881
My sister, she was in here.

57
00:03:37.881 --> 00:03:42.739
That’s what I say, she’s good to talk with.

58
00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:47.057
She keeps a lot of records of things.

59
00:03:47.057 --> 00:03:49.216
His name was Frank.

60
00:03:49.216 --> 00:04:00.552
And I think they had written down the other brother’s name, in which my dad, he, he was a self-taught man.

61
00:04:00.552 --> 00:04:07.030
Matter of fact, he graduated from high school with my oldest brother.

62
00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:09.189
That was in 1965.

63
00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:14.047
So he was well taught and everything, from himself.

64
00:04:14.047 --> 00:04:24.843
And he has a library home right now that has yet to be touched since his passing back in ’07.

65
00:04:24.843 --> 00:04:36.179
So it’s a lot of things back in there that, you know, interest hadn’t brought it up, not up until now.

66
00:04:36.179 --> 00:04:49.674
That I hadn’t even gone back to take a peek at it or going on it, and this Porter guy, I call him T. Porter.

67
00:04:49.674 --> 00:04:53.453
He and Diane, I met them earlier.

68
00:04:53.453 --> 00:05:06.408
They had a deal where, yeah, and Kim, had a deal back in ’95 when they were celebrating the fiftieth year anniversary of Westlake.

69
00:05:06.408 --> 00:05:13.965
My dad, he’s the one that wrote it up on Mossville and the church.

70
00:05:13.965 --> 00:05:26.920
So he has a lot of names in there and they went back through his history, which I’m pretty sure he had documented somewhere.

71
00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:28.000
Yeah.

72
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.140
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s in his library?

73
00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:32.281
<v EDWARDS>I’m sure it is.

74
00:05:32.281 --> 00:05:39.772
Matter of fact, Kim, when this is over with, I’ll get you with Kim.

75
00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:46.728
She had me reading it when you was outside just now, you know.

76
00:05:46.728 --> 00:05:47.263
Yeah.

77
00:05:47.263 --> 00:05:52.079
<v ARSENEAULT>What other sorts of things are in the library?

78
00:05:52.079 --> 00:05:53.684
<v EDWARDS>In his library?

79
00:05:53.684 --> 00:05:54.754
<v ARSENEAULT>Mm hmm.

80
00:05:54.754 --> 00:05:59.570
<v EDWARDS>Besides his sermons, because he was a Baptist minister.

81
00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:05.991
And they always had him do like the history of the town.

82
00:06:05.991 --> 00:06:14.018
Because every year the church, they went through like church’s history and all like this.

83
00:06:14.018 --> 00:06:22.044
So he would dig up names and all the truth there is of it, yeah.

84
00:06:22.044 --> 00:06:26.325
But it made me proud. (laughs) Oh, yeah.

85
00:06:26.325 --> 00:06:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I bet it did.

86
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:29.835
<v EDWARDS>Oh, yes.

87
00:06:29.835 --> 00:06:36.935
And from that, I know they furnished a black baseball team here in this area, you know?

88
00:06:36.935 --> 00:06:40.694
That was real big when I was growing up.

89
00:06:40.694 --> 00:06:44.871
Because I played a little baseball, recreation style, like this.

90
00:06:44.871 --> 00:06:48.212
But that was real big during these times.

91
00:06:48.212 --> 00:06:49.465
Everybody played ball.

92
00:06:49.465 --> 00:06:51.971
Just like the swimming pool here.

93
00:06:51.971 --> 00:07:02.412
Back in my days, well, even today, you tell people you know how to swim and all like this, you know, that was a thing.

94
00:07:02.412 --> 00:07:04.082
Black folks didn’t swim.

95
00:07:04.082 --> 00:07:09.094
I thought from today, but that was something we did every day.

96
00:07:09.094 --> 00:07:09.929
Without fail.

97
00:07:09.929 --> 00:07:14.941
Matter of fact, I used to be one of the first lifeguards.

98
00:07:14.941 --> 00:07:17.447
And you know, worked through here.

99
00:07:17.447 --> 00:07:22.876
So it was real, it was a whole lot of fun growing up.

100
00:07:22.876 --> 00:07:29.976
We did a lot of things that a lot of folks didn’t have the opportunity of doing.

101
00:07:29.976 --> 00:07:30.812
You know?

102
00:07:30.812 --> 00:07:33.318
And they said it was mandatory.

103
00:07:33.318 --> 00:07:37.494
But it was a whole lot of fun growing up.

104
00:07:37.494 --> 00:07:38.747
We enjoyed it.

105
00:07:38.747 --> 00:07:40.000
Most definitely.

106
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.903
<v ARSENEAULT>What kinds of things did you all do for fun?

107
00:07:44.903 --> 00:07:47.845
<v EDWARDS>I was always the sports type.

108
00:07:47.845 --> 00:07:48.826
And bicycles.

109
00:07:48.826 --> 00:07:51.768
Seemed like everybody had a bicycle.

110
00:07:51.768 --> 00:08:02.065
You started working when you were 13, 12, 13 years old, you know, paper routes and stuff like this, you know.

111
00:08:02.065 --> 00:08:03.046
Car wash.

112
00:08:03.046 --> 00:08:08.930
But as far as you know, like cameras or stuff like that,

113
00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:16.285
you’d better be grown before you touched my camera. (laughs) You know, stuff like that.

114
00:08:16.285 --> 00:08:19.227
Marbles, slingshots and stuff like that.

115
00:08:19.227 --> 00:08:26.582
These were the little deals that made our own deals, to where we had fun.

116
00:08:26.582 --> 00:08:32.957
So it didn’t, it didn’t take much to keep us occupied, you know?

117
00:08:32.957 --> 00:08:40.802
I used to go steal one of the neighbor’s horses and ride him bareback, you know.

118
00:08:40.802 --> 00:08:43.254
And that was growing up.

119
00:08:43.254 --> 00:08:44.234
Excuse me.

120
00:08:44.234 --> 00:08:47.176
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s your earliest memory of Mossville?

121
00:08:47.176 --> 00:08:49.628
<v EDWARDS>(laughs) That’s a good one.

122
00:08:49.628 --> 00:08:54.041
This is coming from the top of my head.

123
00:08:54.041 --> 00:09:05.809
The house that we were born and raised over here, I remember that I had to be a toddler around, between one and three.

124
00:09:05.809 --> 00:09:10.713
Sitting on the back porch, which was a dirt porch.

125
00:09:10.713 --> 00:09:14.635
And I would sit there and eat dirt.

126
00:09:14.635 --> 00:09:16.106
All day long.

127
00:09:16.106 --> 00:09:23.461
Just eat dirt. (laughs) Until it got [me up and ran me away from there?].

128
00:09:23.461 --> 00:09:23.952
Yeah.

129
00:09:23.952 --> 00:09:29.836
That was a lot of the fun that we had, you know?

130
00:09:29.836 --> 00:09:35.720
We always had things going on, especially when I became a teenager.

131
00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:37.191
Had different activities.

132
00:09:37.191 --> 00:09:45.036
We called them, now I think they call them Sadie Hawkins dances and stuff like this.

133
00:09:45.036 --> 00:09:48.469
We used to call them record hops.

134
00:09:48.469 --> 00:09:49.940
<v ARSENEAULT>What was one?

135
00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:50.920
<v EDWARDS>Record hop.

136
00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:54.843
Where they could come in and play music.

137
00:09:54.843 --> 00:10:00.727
And all the kids in the neighborhood dance and party and all.

138
00:10:00.727 --> 00:10:08.082
This would be one of the spots right here, when they built this right here.

139
00:10:08.082 --> 00:10:12.495
As far as that, I can remember those things.

140
00:10:12.495 --> 00:10:20.341
But anything that actually stood out other than sports, you know, this was a sports town.

141
00:10:20.341 --> 00:10:21.321
Right here.

142
00:10:21.321 --> 00:10:28.186
Matter of fact, I still go to, I live over in the Lafayette area.

143
00:10:28.186 --> 00:10:37.012
To do, especially to work at a place, a business, and some of the older clientele, like myself,

144
00:10:37.012 --> 00:10:45.348
I can sit down and talk to them about people I played football against from their hometown.

145
00:10:45.348 --> 00:10:48.290
We used to play against them.

146
00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:51.722
And it was a big rivalry deal.

147
00:10:51.722 --> 00:10:56.626
We used to just kick it off all the time.

148
00:10:56.626 --> 00:11:02.019
And that’s between Plaisance, Opelousas, Dequincy, Deridder, Leesville, places like that.

149
00:11:02.019 --> 00:11:03.000
Yeah.

150
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.872
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y’all have a main rival?

151
00:11:05.872 --> 00:11:12.095
<v EDWARDS>I’m going to say our main rival was Ross High School in Crowley.

152
00:11:12.095 --> 00:11:14.489
That’s our main rivalry, yeah.

153
00:11:14.489 --> 00:11:21.190
Every year we had to meet up with them to go to the playoffs.

154
00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:21.669
Yes.

155
00:11:21.669 --> 00:11:23.584
It wasn’t always pretty.

156
00:11:23.584 --> 00:11:27.892
Some of these just won’t let it go. (laughs)

157
00:11:27.892 --> 00:11:29.328
<v ARSENEAULT>It got ugly.

158
00:11:29.328 --> 00:11:30.764
It got ugly?

159
00:11:30.764 --> 00:11:32.679
<v EDWARDS>Yes, it got ugly.

160
00:11:32.679 --> 00:11:36.508
But I mean, it was a fun ugly.

161
00:11:36.508 --> 00:11:38.423
We were very competitive.

162
00:11:38.423 --> 00:11:48.475
We took it out of the heart like you would see, not as talented as a Michael Jordan may have been.

163
00:11:48.475 --> 00:11:51.826
But we took it all to heart.

164
00:11:51.826 --> 00:11:55.177
And everybody put their everything into it.

165
00:11:55.177 --> 00:11:58.049
You know, I mean, we’d practice.

166
00:11:58.049 --> 00:12:01.400
I lived a mile away from here.

167
00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:04.272
You know where the school is.

168
00:12:04.272 --> 00:12:07.623
We used to run from here daily.

169
00:12:07.623 --> 00:12:13.846
After we finished doing our lifeguarding studies and all this, our water safety

170
00:12:13.846 --> 00:12:14.325
<c.p>courses.

171
00:12:14.325 --> 00:12:19.590
We would run from here, go practice football for the summer.

172
00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:26.292
And then after that, we’d run back here and open up for the day.

173
00:12:26.292 --> 00:12:31.557
Mom would bring us a little lunch or something like that.

174
00:12:31.557 --> 00:12:34.430
It was always home, you know.

175
00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:36.344
It was all good.

176
00:12:36.344 --> 00:12:44.003
And doing the, on the weekends, when we weren’t in school, we used to play football.

177
00:12:44.003 --> 00:12:48.311
We used to section off this part of town.

178
00:12:48.311 --> 00:12:55.492
If you get up here to Prater Road going west, that would be like Sulphur.

179
00:12:55.492 --> 00:13:00.279
And from here going east, that would be like Westlake.

180
00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:03.151
That was some very nice residences.

181
00:13:03.151 --> 00:13:11.289
So we would section off, okay, this week we’re going to play against these folks over here.

182
00:13:11.289 --> 00:13:15.597
Of course they had the recreation center over here.

183
00:13:15.597 --> 00:13:22.298
Back on the other end, we used to be in the backyards, you know?

184
00:13:22.298 --> 00:13:25.170
But it was always a riot.

185
00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:28.043
It always was fun, you know.

186
00:13:28.043 --> 00:13:29.000
Always.

187
00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:31.836
<v ARSENEAULT>Could you describe your house?

188
00:13:31.836 --> 00:13:34.104
Where you grew up?

189
00:13:34.104 --> 00:13:34.672
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

190
00:13:34.672 --> 00:13:42.612
I guess you could say it was a three-bedroom, one, two, three, three bedrooms.

191
00:13:42.612 --> 00:13:43.746
Had sisters.

192
00:13:43.746 --> 00:13:45.448
Let me see.

193
00:13:45.448 --> 00:13:48.851
There was eight of us total.

194
00:13:48.851 --> 00:13:51.687
Five girls and three boys.

195
00:13:51.687 --> 00:13:56.224
Of course the boys had the smallest room.

196
00:13:56.224 --> 00:13:59.627
And the girls had one room.

197
00:13:59.627 --> 00:14:03.597
And Mom and Dad had their room.

198
00:14:03.597 --> 00:14:12.104
And right off from their room, it always had a baby bed in that room.

199
00:14:12.104 --> 00:14:20.045
I guess it was expecting new arrivals, to bring them into the force, yeah.

200
00:14:20.045 --> 00:14:21.179
Like that.

201
00:14:21.179 --> 00:14:29.687
It was, you know, I guess you could say growing up we were real close.

202
00:14:29.687 --> 00:14:30.821
Real close.

203
00:14:30.821 --> 00:14:38.761
Matter of fact, it took me till my eighteenth birthday to actually leave home.

204
00:14:38.761 --> 00:14:41.597
Went off to Southern University.

205
00:14:41.597 --> 00:14:44.433
That pretty much was it.

206
00:14:44.433 --> 00:14:50.672
And then from Southern to, Southern University to the air force.

207
00:14:50.672 --> 00:14:54.075
From the air force to Firestone.

208
00:14:54.075 --> 00:14:59.179
And that brings me up to my work career.

209
00:14:59.179 --> 00:14:59.746
Yeah.

210
00:14:59.746 --> 00:15:02.582
Did 35 years with Firestone.

211
00:15:02.582 --> 00:15:08.254
So yeah, I guess you could say it was fun.

212
00:15:08.254 --> 00:15:14.493
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of values did your parents instill in you all?

213
00:15:14.493 --> 00:15:15.627
<v EDWARDS>Oh, shoot.

214
00:15:15.627 --> 00:15:16.761
Oh, man.

215
00:15:16.761 --> 00:15:23.000
Give me an example of what you would call values.

216
00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:28.421
<v ARSENEAULT>Like what did your parents teach you that helped you be successful?

217
00:15:28.421 --> 00:15:33.842
<v EDWARDS>Well from being an observant child, we knew we had to work.

218
00:15:33.842 --> 00:15:42.426
So that’s something, like I say, we started doing ever since we were 12 years old, we started working.

219
00:15:42.426 --> 00:15:49.654
But my first deal that I can think of, taught us how to value a job.

220
00:15:49.654 --> 00:15:52.365
You put everything into your job.

221
00:15:52.365 --> 00:16:00.948
Matter of fact, you’re supposed to be at work for 8 o’clock, between 7 and 7:30 you were there.

222
00:16:00.948 --> 00:16:06.821
You let management know that you can count on me for the day.

223
00:16:06.821 --> 00:16:17.664
There was never no last minute time that you call in and say, “Oh, I’m not going to make it,” or “Something came up.”

224
00:16:17.664 --> 00:16:20.374
There was never none of that.

225
00:16:20.374 --> 00:16:28.054
You know, we would go in and, you know, do what we had to do, you know.

226
00:16:28.054 --> 00:16:35.282
Or you don’t schedule, any time you got work going on, you don’t schedule nothing else.

227
00:16:35.282 --> 00:16:40.704
I mean, I’ve got to go play football, or this and that.

228
00:16:40.704 --> 00:16:46.576
Even though we were good at it, you know, the work came first.

229
00:16:46.576 --> 00:16:51.094
If they needed me to work, we were at work.

230
00:16:51.094 --> 00:16:51.546
Respect.

231
00:16:51.546 --> 00:16:55.612
They gave a lot of respect to the women.

232
00:16:55.612 --> 00:16:56.967
The young ladies.

233
00:16:56.967 --> 00:17:01.485
You wouldn’t catch us beating on women, nothing like that.

234
00:17:01.485 --> 00:17:10.972
A lot of things that they’re going through right now, I don’t even see how they can get away with it.

235
00:17:10.972 --> 00:17:13.682
As far as, people were people.

236
00:17:13.682 --> 00:17:15.489
And that was first.

237
00:17:15.489 --> 00:17:16.393
You know?

238
00:17:16.393 --> 00:17:20.007
That’s what I really learned from my dad.

239
00:17:20.007 --> 00:17:21.362
People are people.

240
00:17:21.362 --> 00:17:29.946
Had an incident, I wish it wouldn’t be brought, you don’t have to broadcast it or anything like that.

241
00:17:29.946 --> 00:17:33.560
But my mom and dad were having problems.

242
00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:41.692
On my dad’s seventieth birthday, he thought he wanted to go out and sow his oats, you know.

243
00:17:41.692 --> 00:17:43.499
Find a nice little

244
00:17:43.499 --> 00:17:46.209
<c.p>young girl and all like this.

245
00:17:46.209 --> 00:17:52.986
And he got out there, it wasn’t like he thought it was going to be.

246
00:17:52.986 --> 00:17:57.052
And he called my mom, wanting to come back.

247
00:17:57.052 --> 00:18:01.118
And everybody in the neighborhood was talking about it.

248
00:18:01.118 --> 00:18:09.249
“You mean to tell me she’s going to take him back after he did her all like this?”

249
00:18:09.249 --> 00:18:11.508
And she made one comment.

250
00:18:11.508 --> 00:18:20.544
She said, “If God can forgive him, so can I.” And that stuck with me pretty much all my life.

251
00:18:20.544 --> 00:18:30.482
If He can, so can I. I think a lot of things they teach us without even having to speak a word.

252
00:18:30.482 --> 00:18:34.096
You know, just following close in their footsteps.

253
00:18:34.096 --> 00:18:35.000
Yeah.

254
00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:36.880
<v ARSENEAULT>A good work ethic.

255
00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:37.820
<v EDWARDS>Well, yeah.

256
00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:41.579
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you all have a garden, growing up?

257
00:18:41.579 --> 00:18:42.049
<v EDWARDS>Garden.

258
00:18:42.049 --> 00:18:42.989
Raised pigs.

259
00:18:42.989 --> 00:18:43.459
Chickens.

260
00:18:43.459 --> 00:18:49.098
My dad had a garden until he became sort of disabled like.

261
00:18:49.098 --> 00:18:52.858
And he had retired from the Firestone plant.

262
00:18:52.858 --> 00:18:57.087
But yeah, he always had a garden going on.

263
00:18:57.087 --> 00:19:03.667
Matter of fact, the gentleman that I was talking to a little bit earlier,

264
00:19:03.667 --> 00:19:09.776
had gray dreadlocks and all like that, he’s a distant relative of mine.

265
00:19:09.776 --> 00:19:14.475
And today was my first time introducing myself to him.

266
00:19:14.475 --> 00:19:17.295
And he’s like eighty-something years old.

267
00:19:17.295 --> 00:19:21.995
So found out we had some of the same deals.

268
00:19:21.995 --> 00:19:24.344
Air force, same growing deals.

269
00:19:24.344 --> 00:19:32.333
Take a hit every once in a while. (laughs) Typical teenagers and all like this, you know.

270
00:19:32.333 --> 00:19:38.443
And like I say, today was my first day exchanging words with him.

271
00:19:38.443 --> 00:19:40.792
Because he was saying hi.

272
00:19:40.792 --> 00:19:43.142
Today was that first day.

273
00:19:43.142 --> 00:19:45.492
I was real interested, too.

274
00:19:45.492 --> 00:19:53.011
And we had a chance, a guy that I idolize, which was my high school coach.

275
00:19:53.011 --> 00:19:57.240
He and my high school coach didn’t get along.

276
00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:03.350
And just from me meeting him and hearing his story and who he

277
00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:12.279
<c.p>was all about, then knowing my coach, I think I’d cut off right hand for each one of them.

278
00:20:12.279 --> 00:20:13.219
You know?

279
00:20:13.219 --> 00:20:14.628
Just like that.

280
00:20:14.628 --> 00:20:25.437
There’s something in Mom getting at me, telling me, “Son, treat everybody as they all,” you know, everybody wasn’t raised in your house.

281
00:20:25.437 --> 00:20:32.016
So they’re not going to have the same views that you have in life.

282
00:20:32.016 --> 00:20:35.306
And I just went on from there.

283
00:20:35.306 --> 00:20:44.705
And like I say, these things come up without them having to even really start talking to us about it.

284
00:20:44.705 --> 00:20:48.934
We didn’t come in and say, “Okay, Dad, Mom.

285
00:20:48.934 --> 00:20:52.224
What about this and what about that?”

286
00:20:52.224 --> 00:20:57.393
Since they’re gone now, we can see what they are saying.

287
00:20:57.393 --> 00:21:04.913
Myself and another sister, Cathy, Mama made the comment to us that we never meet strangers.

288
00:21:04.913 --> 00:21:13.372
And up until lately that never did dawn on me, what in the world was she talking about?

289
00:21:13.372 --> 00:21:14.311
You know?

290
00:21:14.311 --> 00:21:20.421
It’s just that people enjoy sitting down and talking to you, meeting you.

291
00:21:20.421 --> 00:21:23.710
Don’t wish you no harm, no nothing.

292
00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:27.000
And it’s just been a pleasure.

293
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:28.606
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean Mossville?

294
00:21:28.606 --> 00:21:31.819
That’s how it is in Mossville?

295
00:21:31.819 --> 00:21:32.354
<v EDWARDS>Right.

296
00:21:32.354 --> 00:21:37.173
Everybody. (laughs) Everybody had a chance to raise you.

297
00:21:37.173 --> 00:21:39.314
“You’re Sister Helen’s daughter?

298
00:21:39.314 --> 00:21:44.668
Now you know Sister Helen wouldn’t want you doing this.

299
00:21:44.668 --> 00:21:48.416
Come here, I’m going to spank you.

300
00:21:48.416 --> 00:21:55.376
And you tell Sister Helen that I spanked you when you get home.

301
00:21:55.376 --> 00:22:01.801
If not, because I’m going to call her when you get home.”

302
00:22:01.801 --> 00:22:04.478
Everybody raised us, you know?

303
00:22:04.478 --> 00:22:07.155
<v ARSENEAULT>Your dad was a pastor.

304
00:22:07.155 --> 00:22:10.903
Where did you all go to church?

305
00:22:10.903 --> 00:22:12.509
<v EDWARDS>Right up here.

306
00:22:12.509 --> 00:22:14.650
Mount Zion Baptist Church.

307
00:22:14.650 --> 00:22:16.792
He wasn’t the pastor.

308
00:22:16.792 --> 00:22:21.611
He was the pastor at a church in Vinton.

309
00:22:21.611 --> 00:22:22.681
<v ARSENEAULT>In where?

310
00:22:22.681 --> 00:22:23.752
<v EDWARDS>In Vinton.

311
00:22:23.752 --> 00:22:24.288
<v ARSENEAULT>Vinton?

312
00:22:24.288 --> 00:22:24.823
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

313
00:22:24.823 --> 00:22:32.854
And he was one of the associate pastors at Evergreen Baptist Church in Lake Charles.

314
00:22:32.854 --> 00:22:35.531
But he never pastored here.

315
00:22:35.531 --> 00:22:41.956
Like I say, we never got into politics or anything like that.

316
00:22:41.956 --> 00:22:46.239
I’m sure they had their reasons why, whatever.

317
00:22:46.239 --> 00:22:47.845
That was it.

318
00:22:47.845 --> 00:22:51.593
<v ARSENEAULT>And where did you go to school?

319
00:22:51.593 --> 00:22:52.664
<v EDWARDS>Mossville High.

320
00:22:52.664 --> 00:22:54.805
Up the street here.

321
00:22:54.805 --> 00:22:59.088
Matter of fact, from first to twelfth grade.

322
00:22:59.088 --> 00:23:01.765
And then, was it ’69?

323
00:23:01.765 --> 00:23:03.907
I graduated in ’69.

324
00:23:03.907 --> 00:23:10.867
At year ’69 and ’70, they made the school a junior high school.

325
00:23:10.867 --> 00:23:23.717
And whoever went to high school from this area, remember I was saying the west side of Prater Road, they went to Sulphur High.

326
00:23:23.717 --> 00:23:28.000
And this side went to Westlake High.

327
00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:32.806
<v ARSENEAULT>Did it matter like where you wanted to go?

328
00:23:32.806 --> 00:23:37.612
Did they just cut it right down, the community—

329
00:23:37.612 --> 00:23:41.883
<v EDWARDS>They pretty much cut it down the middle.

330
00:23:41.883 --> 00:23:45.621
You had to go here, you know.

331
00:23:45.621 --> 00:23:46.155
Yes.

332
00:23:46.155 --> 00:23:52.563
And that was one of the first times I ran across integration.

333
00:23:52.563 --> 00:24:00.573
They didn’t want my, see, my class was the last class to graduate from Mossville.

334
00:24:00.573 --> 00:24:04.845
And I remember we had to escort my

335
00:24:04.845 --> 00:24:07.515
<c.p>younger sisters to Westlake High.

336
00:24:07.515 --> 00:24:11.786
I mean, it looked like Selma or something.

337
00:24:11.786 --> 00:24:17.126
They didn’t want black folks at the school, you know.

338
00:24:17.126 --> 00:24:23.000
But a lot of our teachers were accepted over there.

339
00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:25.776
<v ARSENEAULT>Teachers who taught here?

340
00:24:25.776 --> 00:24:26.470
<v EDWARDS>Right.

341
00:24:26.470 --> 00:24:29.246
Who taught at Mossville.

342
00:24:29.246 --> 00:24:36.880
Taught the high school, the name may come up, Ms. Rogers.

343
00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:40.350
You may hear her name.

344
00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:43.126
<v ARSENEAULT>What did she teach?

345
00:24:43.126 --> 00:24:43.820
<v EDWARDS>English?

346
00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:45.902
English lit, yeah.

347
00:24:45.902 --> 00:24:52.842
Matter of fact, she failed me my freshman year. (laughs)

348
00:24:52.842 --> 00:24:55.617
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s a good teacher.

349
00:24:55.617 --> 00:24:56.311
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

350
00:24:56.311 --> 00:24:59.781
<v ARSENEAULT>Not afraid to fail students.

351
00:24:59.781 --> 00:25:01.169
<v EDWARDS>That’s right.

352
00:25:01.169 --> 00:25:04.639
She was a good teacher.

353
00:25:04.639 --> 00:25:05.333
Yes.

354
00:25:05.333 --> 00:25:08.109
But it’s been interesting.

355
00:25:08.109 --> 00:25:10.191
It’s been interesting.

356
00:25:10.191 --> 00:25:14.355
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite teacher?

357
00:25:14.355 --> 00:25:15.743
<v EDWARDS>Um, favorite.

358
00:25:15.743 --> 00:25:20.601
I guess you could say Coach Williams.

359
00:25:20.601 --> 00:25:27.541
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that the coach Mr. Haki didn’t get along with?

360
00:25:27.541 --> 00:25:28.235
<v EDWARDS>Right.

361
00:25:28.235 --> 00:25:31.011
He was my favorite.

362
00:25:31.011 --> 00:25:44.891
Because at the time that he came into my life, until afterwards, you know, he always was the same person.

363
00:25:44.891 --> 00:25:47.667
Didn’t portray anything different.

364
00:25:47.667 --> 00:25:56.689
We’re here for a job and this is what we’re going not do.

365
00:25:56.689 --> 00:26:02.240
He was a motivator and all, you know.

366
00:26:02.240 --> 00:26:02.934
Yeah.

367
00:26:02.934 --> 00:26:10.568
He would be the guy that stands out in my life.

368
00:26:10.568 --> 00:26:13.344
<v ARSENEAULT>Is he still alive?

369
00:26:13.344 --> 00:26:14.038
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

370
00:26:14.038 --> 00:26:18.896
It’s a wonder he’s not here today.

371
00:26:18.896 --> 00:26:21.672
Because he’s usually here.

372
00:26:21.672 --> 00:26:25.142
Maybe that’s why I’m here.

373
00:26:25.142 --> 00:26:30.000
Give him a break, huh? (laughs)

374
00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:35.557
<v ARSENEAULT>Gosh, I feel like we could talk all day about Mossville.

375
00:26:35.557 --> 00:26:49.196
I’m just going to ask you some wrap-up questions and then we can get into more detail when we come to your parents’ house for an interview.

376
00:26:49.196 --> 00:26:49.701
<v EDWARDS>Okay.

377
00:26:49.701 --> 00:26:55.258
<v ARSENEAULT>I wanted to ask you, what does home mean to you?

378
00:26:55.258 --> 00:26:59.804
<v EDWARDS>Home is where you’re accepted, when nobody else will.

379
00:26:59.804 --> 00:27:03.845
That’s as simple as I can put it.

380
00:27:03.845 --> 00:27:08.897
Nobody having an agenda for you or anything like that.

381
00:27:08.897 --> 00:27:16.979
Just hey, until you can get up on your feet and do better, you’re welcome here.

382
00:27:16.979 --> 00:27:19.000
Yep, that’s it.

383
00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.040
<v ARSENEAULT>And what about community?

384
00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:24.100
What does that mean to you?

385
00:27:24.100 --> 00:27:27.669
<v EDWARDS>Well, you grow, experience, do things together.

386
00:27:27.669 --> 00:27:32.259
You know, you’re still on one accord, you know?

387
00:27:32.259 --> 00:27:41.948
Like your kids, bring on your legacy, things that you, you know, that you teach them, that you learn.

388
00:27:41.948 --> 00:27:45.518
Sort of like in sports or education.

389
00:27:45.518 --> 00:27:52.657
Of course you always want your kids to do better than what you’ve done.

390
00:27:52.657 --> 00:27:55.717
That’s one thing that I push.

391
00:27:55.717 --> 00:28:00.817
And so far, I’ve been that much successful, you know.

392
00:28:00.817 --> 00:28:04.386
Five kids, four girls and one boy.

393
00:28:04.386 --> 00:28:10.506
And all of them going off and made a name for themselves.

394
00:28:10.506 --> 00:28:11.526
Excuse me.

395
00:28:11.526 --> 00:28:16.116
Neither one has been in any kind of trouble.

396
00:28:16.116 --> 00:28:21.215
Cops have never been called for them, anything like that.

397
00:28:21.215 --> 00:28:22.745
Illegitimate children, none.

398
00:28:22.745 --> 00:28:30.904
It’s been, I’m not going to say a roller coaster ride, other than the fun. (laughs)

399
00:28:30.904 --> 00:28:33.454
<v ARSENEAULT>All of the good stuff.

400
00:28:33.454 --> 00:28:36.004
None of the bad stuff.

401
00:28:36.004 --> 00:28:36.514
<v EDWARDS>Right.

402
00:28:36.514 --> 00:28:37.024
Right.

403
00:28:37.024 --> 00:28:38.554
<v ARSENEAULT>No throwing up.

404
00:28:38.554 --> 00:28:39.064
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

405
00:28:39.064 --> 00:28:42.633
I remember one deal, like Hurricane Audrey.

406
00:28:42.633 --> 00:28:45.693
You all familiar with Katrina, right?

407
00:28:45.693 --> 00:28:50.793
The one that was worse than that was Hurricane Audrey.

408
00:28:50.793 --> 00:28:53.343
And we had to uproot

409
00:28:53.343 --> 00:29:04.052
<c.p>from the houses and go down to the school where you walk through the door, the whole community was in there.

410
00:29:04.052 --> 00:29:09.151
And you just wasn’t aware of danger, anything like that.

411
00:29:09.151 --> 00:29:11.191
We’d go play. (laughs)

412
00:29:11.191 --> 00:29:11.701
<v ARSENEAULT>Barbecue.

413
00:29:11.701 --> 00:29:15.781
<v EDWARDS>Right on. (laughs) Now you said it wrong.

414
00:29:15.781 --> 00:29:18.841
It’s barbecue [not pronouncing the “r.”]

415
00:29:18.841 --> 00:29:19.351
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

416
00:29:19.351 --> 00:29:19.861
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

417
00:29:19.861 --> 00:29:27.000
I don't know where they come up with that word barbecue, you know.

418
00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:28.628
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s not right.

419
00:29:28.628 --> 00:29:32.969
<v EDWARDS>No. (laughs) See, we’re so close to Texas.

420
00:29:32.969 --> 00:29:43.279
I don't know if you notice the accent between folks, between Texas and Lake Charles is about the same.

421
00:29:43.279 --> 00:29:50.876
But the more you start going east is more of a Cajun, you know.

422
00:29:50.876 --> 00:29:59.558
This is in the ‘80s, I told a guy, I forget how I called his name.

423
00:29:59.558 --> 00:30:04.442
Mr. Leger, you know “What you call me, boy?

424
00:30:04.442 --> 00:30:07.155
Mr. Leger [like ledger]., huh?”

425
00:30:07.155 --> 00:30:08.240
Mr. Ledger?

426
00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:15.295
“My name is Leger, boy!” [with Cajun pronunciation] Stuff like that, you know.

427
00:30:15.295 --> 00:30:15.837
<v ARSENEAULT>Cajun.

428
00:30:15.837 --> 00:30:16.922
<v EDWARDS>Oh, yeah.

429
00:30:16.922 --> 00:30:17.465
Cajun.

430
00:30:17.465 --> 00:30:27.233
And then to see, they say God does things for a reason, he always does things for reasons.

431
00:30:27.233 --> 00:30:39.171
When I moved to the Lafayette area in ’85, ’85 or ’86, families, I’m talking about husband and wife and your kids,

432
00:30:39.171 --> 00:30:48.938
you all would go out and celebrate at a club, like a zydeco dance or something like that.

433
00:30:48.938 --> 00:30:52.736
You all would be at the party.

434
00:30:52.736 --> 00:30:58.163
See, we didn’t do that when we were growing up.

435
00:30:58.163 --> 00:31:01.961
It was just, you know your place.

436
00:31:01.961 --> 00:31:04.132
Nothing but kids here.

437
00:31:04.132 --> 00:31:04.674
Okay.

438
00:31:04.674 --> 00:31:06.845
Your kids do this.

439
00:31:06.845 --> 00:31:13.357
And your grown folks, they had their section, their times and all.

440
00:31:13.357 --> 00:31:16.612
I guess that’s all and good.

441
00:31:16.612 --> 00:31:29.093
Grandpa don’t want his grandkids to see him get sloppy drunk or anything like that, no. Beat his wife or anything like that.

442
00:31:29.093 --> 00:31:31.806
But it’s all been fun.

443
00:31:31.806 --> 00:31:36.147
And I can say it’s a good experience.

444
00:31:36.147 --> 00:31:38.860
I still learn things today.

445
00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:47.000
Or it comes back and is it justified to me, why this and that.

446
00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:55.224
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s the most important thing you want people to remember about Mossville that isn’t—

447
00:31:55.224 --> 00:31:55.812
<v EDWARDS>Family.

448
00:31:55.812 --> 00:31:57.574
Family is love.

449
00:31:57.574 --> 00:31:59.924
That’s the only thing.

450
00:31:59.924 --> 00:32:08.148
That’s the only thing I think that is genuine that you can pass on.

451
00:32:08.148 --> 00:32:09.323
Family’s love.

452
00:32:09.323 --> 00:32:12.848
I mean, there’s nothing like it.

453
00:32:12.848 --> 00:32:16.372
That’s solves a lot of problems.

454
00:32:16.372 --> 00:32:21.072
Keeps a lot of things on balance, too.

455
00:32:21.072 --> 00:32:22.834
Man, you busy.

456
00:32:22.834 --> 00:32:24.009
<v ARSENEAULT>Mm hmm.

457
00:32:24.009 --> 00:32:30.471
<v EDWARDS>You move like a little roach. (laughter) Didn’t mess up nothing.

458
00:32:30.471 --> 00:32:32.233
Nothing blown around.

459
00:32:32.233 --> 00:32:42.807
I wouldn’t have said you move—(laughs) I thought they looking for the exits or something, what’s going on?

460
00:32:42.807 --> 00:32:44.570
Sorry about that.

461
00:32:44.570 --> 00:32:48.682
<v ARSENEAULT>We’re not supposed to laugh on tape.

462
00:32:48.682 --> 00:32:49.269
<v EDWARDS>No.

463
00:32:49.269 --> 00:32:51.619
<v ARSENEAULT>You can’t make jokes.

464
00:32:51.619 --> 00:32:54.556
<v EDWARDS>I wasn’t supposed to, either?

465
00:32:54.556 --> 00:32:58.668
<v ARSENEAULT>No. You can do whatever you want.

466
00:32:58.668 --> 00:32:59.843
<v EDWARDS>Oh, okay.

467
00:32:59.843 --> 00:33:01.018
I’m sorry.

468
00:33:01.018 --> 00:33:03.368
